the u.s and saudi arabia have deep ties  that go back  decades ties that connect washington and  riyadh  and both countries economies the  countries are so intertwined  economically  that washington is often reluctant to  distance itself from riyadh politically  the current tension over the death of  washington post journalist  jamal khashoggi is no exception here's a  look at some of the glue  that's binding the two powers together  first  saudi arabia and the u.s are connected  by oil  the u.s has doubled production of crude  over the past decade and is now  considered the world's largest producer  but it still looks to saudi arabia for  nearly 10 percent of its petroleum  imports  and there's more saudi arabia holds the  power to quickly influence global oil  prices  some call it the central bank of oil  that's because in saudi arabia the state  controls oil production  which allows for a more streamlined  operation than in countries like the us  where hundreds of private companies work  independently  across numerous states the kingdom has  the power to turn the flow  up and down quickly impacting gas prices  inflation and global growth saudi  financiers have also forged  close relationships with american  companies especially in tech and finance  that's because crown prince mohammed bin  salman has set the ambitious goal of  making saudi arabia  a major investment hub in an effort to  diversify the country's economy beyond  oil  using the country's public investment  fund the saudis have  directly invested 3.5 billion dollars in  uber  which gave pif's chief a seat on the  company's board  taking a roughly five percent stake in  tesla which fueled speculation that it  could take over the company someday  and committed 45 billion dollars to a  tech fund managed by softbank  a japanese firm that's investing heavily  in u.s startups like wework  since 2016 the kingdom has become the  biggest single source of funding for u.s  startups  thais are also growing stronger with  wall street pif committed  up to 20 billion dollars for an  infrastructure fund managed by  blackstone  and blackrock the world's largest asset  manager  has met with the crown prince about  possible investment partnerships  and is planning to open an office in  saudi arabia however there's one caveat  some of saudi arabia's ambitious  business plans have been put on hold  due to the stalled ipo of saudi aramco  the country's massive state-owned oil  company  then there are the arms and defense  deals for decades the u.s has provided  military support for saudi arabia  and the kingdom has purchased tens of  billions of dollars of weapons and  defense systems from the united states  the u.s defense industry is so  entrenched in saudi arabia that some  american companies are establishing  joint ventures  to make and repair equipment in saudi  arabia  in 2017 the u.s and saudi arabia  announced an  arms deal they said could eventually be  worth 110  billion dollars that's peanuts for you  should have increased it  these contracts some of which have yet  to be approved would benefit a handful  of u.s companies  lockheed martin said in a statement that  it amassed 28 billion dollars in  potential new business from saudi arabia  including the planned sale of combat  ships and helicopters that would be  assembled at a new plant  in saudi arabia creating 450 jobs there  it also struck a deal to sell its thaad  missile defense system to the kingdom  raytheon said it was setting up a new  saudi-based defense arm  encompassing munitions and cyber  security and boeing was approved to sell  saudi arabia  seven billion dollars in chinook and  apache helicopters  and it announced a joint venture in  march that would create 6 000  saudi jobs boeing has said its share of  the planned deals could be worth as much  as  50 billion dollars trump has made it  clear he does not want to jeopardize  deals like these  even in light of political controversies  if we don't sell it to them they'll say  well thank you very much we'll buy it  from russia  or thank you very much we'll buy it from  china so what good does that do us  there are other things we can do some  u.s business leaders have distanced  themselves from the kingdom after  khashoggi's disappearance  by pulling out of the saudi conference  dubbed davos in the desert but the  country's economic ties  run deep and the alliance has proven to  be a resilient one
 

